From d11e0361145680fec5f4ba44eebd9472accf1c72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:29:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] feat(installer): install from a bundletool APK set what
 the device gets

An .apks from bundletool build-apks holds alternatives, not one install:
standalone APKs per ABI for old SDK levels, a split variant per SDK range
(base-master.apk, base-master_2.apk), instant APKs, ABI/density/language
splits for every device, and on-demand modules and asset packs. the other runtime
took every .apk in it, so such a set failed with "Multiple base APKs" and
docs/installer.md asked users to export a single matching set first.

Read the set's toc.pb (BuildApksResult, bundletool's commands.proto) and
choose as bundletool install-apks does (ApkMatcher, VariantMatcher and
the matcher of each dimension, cited in ApkSetToc): the one non-instant
variant without SDK runtime that matches the other runtime's SDK level and ABIs
(NativeLibs.SUPPORTED, x86_64 then arm64-v8a); its install-time modules
with their dependencies (the deprecated on-demand flag for sets older
than bundletool 0.10.2); the APKs of those modules that match; and the
install-time asset slices. A set with no compatible variant fails with
INSTALL_FAILED_NO_MATCHING_ABIS or INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK, and device
tier targeting, which bundletool refuses without a tier in the device
spec, is refused too.

Density and language splits are all kept: the other runtime's density is chosen
per launch and its languages follow Windows and per-app locales, with no
store to deliver a missing split later. The matcher implements both
dimensions (Device with a density and locales) and is tested with them,
so this is a choice of the device description in PackageBundles.Device.
Without toc.pb (.xapk, .apkm, .apk+) every .apk entry is still the
install. The icon handler reads the same APKs.

PackageBundlesTest builds a set laid out as build-apks lays it out and
checks the selection for the other runtime's device, a full device spec (arm64-v8a,
420 dpi, Hebrew), SDK 28 and SDK 19 devices, the density choice at eight
densities, and refusals: unsupported ABIs, too old an SDK, a toc naming a
missing APK, a truncated toc.pb and device tiers. SplitPackageContractTest
installs an APK set whose standalone variant is another base APK and
expects the installed split cluster, unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
